't size the funding gap against debt capacity.\n- **Spreads:** Utility bond spreads widened 30bp in Q3 2026 to 165bp. That makes debt more expensive, but it hasn't shut the market.\n- **Issuance:** Norvane priced a C$1.1B bond comfortably, so investment-grade debt access is working for at least the stronger name. Equity is weaker. Two utility share offerings were cut back, and Pellham withdrew its C$2.4B equity offering after demand fell short.\n- **Pellham's response:** It said it will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and defer two substation programmes until financing improves. This is the strongest evidence that debt alone isn't covering Pellham's plan. Pellham is the largest single buyer of grid equipment in Caldria.\n- **Leverage and ratings:** I found no data on leverage, FFO/debt or ratings. That is a gap. Pellham's choice to raise equity and then cut spending, rather than issue more debt, suggests it is close to its balance-sheet or ratings limits. That is an inference, not something the sources state.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n- Norvane looks able to fund its +12% plan mostly with debt.\n- Pellham is constrained and is cutting its plan. Expect the 2027 capex to come in below the stated plan.\n\nThis matters for grid-equipment demand, because Pellham's order cuts are a downside risk to it.","ttft_ms":2726,"type":"result","duration_ms":22419,"uuid":"ad2ff52f-3264-4d60-9518-516af89bb164","ttft_stream_ms":1928,"time_to_request_ms":420,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":33,"system_prompt":28,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":17,"input_attachments":87,"input_hooks":25,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":20,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":35,"init_frame_send":12,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":23,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":0,"autocompact":12,"request_announcements":48,"query_setup":1,"model_gates":8,"tool_schema_build":12,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":14,"request_build":40},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_